<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gim Man-deok</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1739">1739</a>–<a href="/years/1812">1812</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Kim Man-deok, Man-deok halmang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gim Man-deok was a Korean merchant of Joseon from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeju-island">Jeju Island</a> who used her fortune to feed the island in a famine. Orphaned young, she became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kisaeng">gisaeng</a>; at 22 she had her name removed from the gisaeng registry, regained her yangmin status and opened an inn for merchants and a commission agency for trade between Jeju and the mainland, becoming by 50 one of the two richest people in Joseon. When famine struck Jeju from 1792 and 170,000 people died of starvation, she imported and distributed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, saving 18,000 lives. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeongjo-of-joseon">King Jeongjo</a> granted her wish to visit the capital and Mount <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geum-river">Geumgang</a>, though Jeju women had been forbidden to leave the island for 200 years; she left her belongings to the poor.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gim Man-deok was born in 1739, in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeongjo-of-joseon">King Yeongjo</a>, into a poor household on Jeju. Her father, Kim Eung-ryeol, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangban">yangban</a> of the Gimhae Kim clan living in political exile, and her mother was a yangmin of the Jeju Go clan. Because the children of a union between two classes took the lower status, she and her two older brothers were yangmin. Jeju's matrifocal culture gave women more economic and social independence than the rest of Joseon allowed.</p><p>Orphaned when her father died in a storm on his way back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naju">Naju</a> and her mother died soon after, she was taken in by the gisaeng Wol Jung-seon as an adoptive daughter. Besides singing and dancing she learned the arts, medicine, crafts and horse riding, and, since the gisaeng houses stood near the market, the ways of trade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kisaeng">Gisaeng</a> had been state property since 1650 and had to pay heavily for their freedom. Once free, she dealt in Jeju goods such as horsehair, seaweed, ear shell, ox bezoar, pearls, cloth and cosmetics, and bought grain on the mainland to sell on the island. Using her knowledge of the tax laws she gained a monopoly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and salt.</p><p>The famine that began in 1792 with repeated crop failures and floods grew worse in 1794 with strong winds and high tides. The governor of Jeju asked the court for 20,000 bags of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, but seven of the twelve boats sent were wrecked on the way. Gim spent most of her fortune on the food she brought in. Besides granting her wish to travel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeongjo-of-joseon">King Jeongjo</a> gave her an honorary title and the post of head nurse of the palace dispensary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She returned to Jeju in 1797 and died there in 1812, aged 73, leaving all she had to the poor except a small sum for an orphan she had adopted. Pak Chega and Chŏng Yagyong wrote poems in her honour, and the Chief State Councilor Ch'ae Chegong wrote her biography, Man-deok jeon, praising her virtue and charity. She has been called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>'s first CEO. A museum to her opened on Jeju in 1978, and the island awards the Kim Man-deok Award each year to two women of exemplary conduct.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gim Man-deok” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some won lasting fame: the painter Sin Saimdang, mother of the philosopher Yi I; the gisaeng poet Hwang Jini; and the merchant <b>Kim Man-deok</b>, who gave her rice to the starving.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Joseon. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeju-island">Jeju Island</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.38,126.55&amp;year=1786" aria-label="Jeju Island on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Gim Man-deok"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5467 4698h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5467 4698h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=33.38,126.55&amp;year=1786">What was here in 1786? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
